## Why This Is Asked

This question tests the ability to design extensible components using the Strategy and Registry patterns, along with understanding of thread-safety considerations and basic testing approaches for eviction correctness.

## Key Concepts

- **Strategy Pattern**: Encapsulate different eviction algorithms as interchangeable plug-ins
- **Runtime Registry**: Enable adding new policies without modifying existing code or restarting
- **Thread-Safety**: Considerations for concurrent access in JavaScript environments
- **Testing**: Basic approaches to verify eviction correctness

## Code Example

```typescript
interface EvictionPolicy<K> {
  onAccess(key: K, cache: Map<K, unknown>): void;
  onInsert(key: K, value: unknown, cache: Map<K, unknown>): void;
  evict(cache: Map<K, unknown>): K | null;
}

class PolicyRegistry<K> {
  private byName = new Map<string, EvictionPolicy<K>>();

  register(name: string, policy: EvictionPolicy<K>): void {
    this.byName.set(name, policy);
  }

  get(name: string): EvictionPolicy<K> | undefined {
    return this.byName.get(name);
  }
}

class Cache<K, V> {
  private store = new Map<K, V>();
  private registry: PolicyRegistry<K>;
  private policy: EvictionPolicy<K>;

  constructor(policy: EvictionPolicy<K>, registry?: PolicyRegistry<K>) {
    this.policy = policy;
    this.registry = registry || new PolicyRegistry();
  }

  set(key: K, value: V): void {
    if (this.store.size >= this.capacity) {
      const evicted = this.policy.evict(this.store);
      if (evicted) this.store.delete(evicted);
    }
    this.policy.onInsert(key, value, this.store);
    this.store.set(key, value);
  }

  get(key: K): V | undefined {
    const value = this.store.get(key);
    if (value !== undefined) {
      this.policy.onAccess(key, value, this.store);
    }
    return value;
  }

  registerPolicy(name: string, policy: EvictionPolicy<K>): void {
    this.registry.register(name, policy);
  }
}

// Usage
const lruPolicy: EvictionPolicy<string> = {
  onAccess: (key, cache) => { /* update access order */ },
  onInsert: (key, value, cache) => { /* track insertion */ },
  evict: (cache) => { /* return least recently used key */ }
};

const cache = new Cache(lruPolicy);
cache.set('a', 1);
cache.get('a');
```

## Trade-offs

| Policy | Pros | Cons |
|--------|------|------|
| **LRU** | Good hit rate for repeated access | Overhead tracking access order |
| **FIFO** | Simple, low overhead | Ignores access patterns |
| **TTL** | Automatic expiration, good for time-sensitive data | Requires background cleanup |

## Why This Is Asked

This question tests the open-closed principle, runtime extensibility, and clean separation between flag evaluation and rollout strategies. It also evaluates the ability to design for multiple concurrent experiments with real-world constraints.

## Key Concepts

- **Strategy Pattern**: Enables extensible rollout logic where new strategies can be added without modifying existing code
- **Runtime Registry**: Provides plugin-like extensibility, allowing new strategies to be registered dynamically
- **Thread-Safe Lazy Initialization**: Ensures safe concurrent access and defers expensive computations until needed
- **Minimal TypeScript Interfaces**: Small surface area reduces complexity and maintenance burden
- **Context-Based Evaluation**: Makes decisions using user attributes, device information, and geographic data

## Code Example

```typescript
interface FlagContext {
  userId?: string;
  device?: string;
  region?: string;
  attributes?: Record<string, unknown>;
}

interface RolloutStrategy {
  name: string;
  evaluate(context: FlagContext, config: RolloutConfig): boolean;
}

interface RolloutConfig {
  percentage?: number;
  segment?: string;
  shardKey?: string;
}

class StrategyRegistry {
  private strategies = new Map<string, () => RolloutStrategy>();

  register(name: string, factory: () => RolloutStrategy): void {
    this.strategies.set(name, factory);
  }

  get(name: string): RolloutStrategy {
    const factory = this.strategies.get(name);
    if (!factory) throw new Error(`Strategy '${name}' not found`);
    return factory();
  }
}

class FeatureFlagEngine {
  private registry: StrategyRegistry;
  private strategyCache = new Map<string, RolloutStrategy>();

  constructor(registry: StrategyRegistry) {
    this.registry = registry;
  }

  evaluate(flag: string, context: FlagContext, config: RolloutConfig): boolean {
    const strategy = this.getStrategy(config.strategy);
    return strategy.evaluate(context, config);
  }

  private getStrategy(name: string): RolloutStrategy {
    if (!this.strategyCache.has(name)) {
      this.strategyCache.set(name, this.registry.get(name));
    }
    return this.strategyCache.get(name)!;
  }
}
```

## Realistic Scenario: Gradual UI Module Rollout

A product team wants to roll out a new checkout flow to 10% of users, then gradually increase to 50% while targeting English-speaking regions first:

```typescript
